auth required)\nGET /api/queries/timeseries - Timeseries query (auth required)\nGET /api/queries/breakdown - Breakdown query (auth required)\n\nGET/POST /api/dashboards - List/Create dashboards (auth required)\nGET/PUT/DELETE /api/dashboards/:id - Get/Update/Delete dashboard (auth required)\n\nGET /api/exports/events - Export events (auth required)\nGET /api/exports/dashboards/:id - Export dashboard (auth required)\n\nLet me think about the query parameters for the query engine:\n- count: ?name=page_view&from=2024-01-01&to=2024-12-31\n- timeseries: ?name=page_view&from=2024-01-01&to=2024-12-31&bucket=daily (or daily, hourly)\n- breakdown: ?name=page_view&property=url&from=2024-01-01&to=2024-12-31\n\nFor exports: ?format=csv|json\n\nLet me think about password hashing. Since we don't have bcrypt in deps, I'll use Node.js built-in crypto.scryptSync.\n\nOK let me also think about the \"createApp\" export requirement. I'll create `src/app.ts` with a `createApp` function.\n\nLet me also think about test infrastructure. The task says \"you are encouraged to write your own tests\" but doesn't require them. The timeout is 30 minutes so I want to be efficient.\n\nActually wait, I need to check if `node_modules` is already installed.\n\nLet me start coding. I'll create all the files.\n\nLet me think about the structure more carefully.\n\nFor the DB module, I need to handle the SQLite database creation. The schema.sql is provided but I'll need to initialize it in code.\n\nFor password hashing, I'll use crypto.pbkdf2Sync or crypto.scryptSync since that's built-in.\n\nFor JWT, I'll use a simple secret key.\n\nLet me think about the route handlers:\n\n1. User Registration\n   - POST /api/users/register\n   - Body: { email, password, tenantId? }\n   - If tenantId not provided, create a new tenant\n   - Hash password, insert user\n   - Return JWT\n\nActually, looking at the schema more carefully, there's no separate tenants table. The tenant_id is just a string on users and events. So tenants are implicit - they're just groups identified by a tenant_id string.\n\nFor registration, I'll have the client specify a tenant_id, or I can auto-generate one. Let me allow the user to specify a tenant_id, and if not provided, use a generated UUID.\n\nActually for a simpler approach, let me have the registration accept a tenant_id (or generate one), and the first user in a tenant becomes admin. Let me think...\n\nActually, looking at the requirement: \"Each user belongs to a tenant (organization).\" So users join tenants. Let me make it so registration requires a tenant_id. The first user in a tenant becomes admin automatically.\n\nWait actually, let me re-read: \"Users need to register accounts, log in, and receive authentication tokens.\" - Registration implies self-service. I'll generate a tenant_id if one isn't provided.\n\nLet me create a clean structure:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  app.ts       - Main Express app with createApp()\n  db.ts        - Database initialization\n  middleware/\n    auth.ts    - JWT auth middleware\n  routes/\n    auth.ts    - Auth routes (register, login)\n    users.ts   - User routes (profile, role management)\n    events.ts  - Event ingestion\n    queries.ts - Query engine\n    dashboards.ts - Dashboard CRUD\n    exports.ts - Data export\n```\n\nActually, let me simplify and put it all in fewer files to reduce complexity. Let me use:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  app.ts       - Express app, routes, all logic\n  db.ts        - Database\n  middleware/\n    auth.ts    - Auth middleware\n```\n\nActually, that's too few files. Let me find a good balance. I'll go with:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  app.ts       - Express app setup, creates and exports createApp()\n  db.ts        - Database initialization and helpers\n  middleware.ts - Auth middleware\n  routes.ts    - All route handlers\n  utils.ts     - Password hashing, JWT helpers, export helpers\n```\n\nThis is cleaner. Let me code it up.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. I want to make sure the code is well-organized and passes lint/build. Let me plan each file:\n\n### src/db.ts\n- Initialize database from schema.sql\n- Export the database instance\n- Run the schema SQL on startup\n\n### src/utils.ts  \n- hashPassword(password: string): string\n- verifyPassword(password: string, hash: string): boolean\n- generateToken(payload): string\n- verifyToken(token: string): payload\n\n### src/middleware.ts  \n- requireAuth middleware - extracts and verifies JWT, sets req.user\n\n### src/routes.ts\nAll route handlers, organized by feature area.\n\n### src/app.ts\nExpress app setup, body parser, mount routes, export createApp\n\nLet me implement this now.\n\nFor the password hashing, I'll use:\n```typescript\nimport * as crypto from 'crypto';\n\nfunction hashPassword(password: string): string {\n  const salt = crypto.randomBytes(16).toString('hex');\n  const hash = crypto.scryptSync(password, salt, 64).toString('hex');\n  return `${salt}:${hash}`;\n}\n\nfunction verifyPassword(password: string, stored: string): boolean {\n  const [salt, hash] = stored.split(':');\n  const verify = crypto.scryptSync(password, salt, 64).toString('hex');\n  return hash === verify;\n}\n```\n\nFor JWT:\n```typescript\nimport jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';\n\nconst SECRET = 'analytics-dashboard-secret-key-2024';\n\nfunction generateToken(payload: object): string {\n  return jwt.sign(payload, SECRET, { expiresIn: '24h' });\n}\n\nfunction verifyToken(token: string): any {\n  return jwt.verify(token, SECRET);\n}\n```\n\nFor the database, I'll read schema.sql and execute it.\n\nLet me think about the Express request type extension for attaching user info.\n\nI need to extend the Express Request interface to add user:\n\n```typescript\ndeclare global {\n  namespace Express {\n    interface Request {\n      user?: {\n        id: string;\n        email: string;\n        tenantId: string;\n        role: string;\n      };\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOK let me also think about the routes more carefully.\n\n### Auth routes:\n\nPOST /api/auth/register\n```json\n{\n  \"email\": \"user@example.com\",\n  \"password\": \"password123\",\n  \"tenantId\": \"optional-org-id\"  // if omitted, generated\n}\n```\nResponse: { token, user: { id, email, tenantId, role } }\n\nPOST /api/auth/login\n```json\n{\n  \"email\": \"user@example.com\",\n  \"password\": \"password123\"\n}\n```\nResponse: { token, user: { id, email, tenantId, role } }\n\n### User routes:\n\nGET /api/users/profile\nResponse: { id, email, tenantId, role, createdAt }\n\nPATCH /api/users/:id/role (admin only)\n```json\n{\n  \"role\": \"editor\"\n}\n```\nResponse: { id, email, role }\n\n### Event routes:\n\nPOST /api/events\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"page_view\",\n  \"properties\": { \"url\": \"/home\", \"browser\": \"chrome\" },\n  \"timestamp\": \"2024-01-01T00:00:00Z\"  // optional, defaults to now\n}\n```\nResponse: { id, name, tenantId, timestamp }\n\nPOST /api/events/batch\n```json\n{\n  \"events\": [\n    { \"name\": \"page_view\", \"properties\": { \"url\": \"/home\" } },\n    { \"name\": \"click\", \"properties\": { \"element\": \"button\" } }\n  ]\n}\n```\nResponse: { count: 2, ids: [...] }\n\n### Query routes:\n\nGET /api/queries/count?name=page_view&from=...&to=...\nResponse: { count: 123 }\n\nGET /api/queries/timeseries?name=page_view&from=...&to=...&bucket=daily\nResponse: { results: [{ bucket: \"2024-01-01\", count: 10 }, ...] }\n\nGET /api/queries/breakdown?name=page_view&property=url&from=...&to=...\nResponse: { results: [{ value: \"/home\", count: 50 }, ...] }\n\n### Dashboard routes:\n\nGET /api/dashboards\nResponse: { dashboards: [...] }\n\nPOST /api/dashboards\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"My Dashboard\",\n  \"config\": { ... },\n  \"widgets\": [\n    { \"type\": \"counter\", \"config\": { \"eventName\": \"page_view\" }, ... }\n  ]\n}\n```\nResponse: { id, name, ... }\n\nGET /api/dashboards/:id\nResponse: { id, name, config, widgets: [...] }\n\nPUT /api/dashboards/:id\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Updated Name\",\n  \"config\": { ... }\n}\n```\nResponse: { id, name, config, ... }\n\nDELETE /api/dashboards/:id\nResponse: { success: true }\n\n### Export routes:\n\nGET /api/exports/events?format=csv&name=page_view&from=...&to=...\nResponse: CSV file or JSON array\n\nGET /api/exports/dashboards/:id?format=json\nResponse: Dashboard data with widgets in JSON or CSV\n\nNow let me implement this. I'll write all the source files.\n\nOne more consideration: I need to extend the Express Request type. I can do this in the middleware file or in a separate types file, or just at the top of routes.ts.\n\nLet me think about the `createApp` function signature. It should return an Express.Application, and it may optionally accept the database path.\n\n```typescript\nexport function createApp(dbPath?: string): Express.Application\n```\n\nLet me now write all the code. I want to be careful about TypeScript strict mode.\n\nFor the batch event processing, I'll use a transaction in better-sqlite3.\n\nLet me also think about the event schema. The schema says:\n- id TEXT PRIMARY KEY\n- tenant_id TEXT NOT NULL\n- name TEXT NOT NULL\n- properties TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'\n- timestamp TEXT NOT NULL\n- user_id TEXT\n- session_id TEXT\n\nSo properties is stored as a JSON string.\n\nFor the query engine:\n- Count: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM events WHERE tenant_id = ? AND name = ? AND timestamp BETWEEN ? AND ?\n- Timeseries: Using strftime for bucketing\n  - daily: strftime('%Y-%m-%d', timestamp)\n  - hourly: strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:00:00', timestamp)\n- Breakdown: SELECT json_extract(properties, '$.url') as value, COUNT(*) FROM events WHERE ... GROUP BY value\n\nSQLite json_extract should work.\n\nLet me code it all up now.\n\nActually, one concern: module resolution is Node16, which means I need to use proper extensions in imports. With Node16 module resolution, you need to include the file extension in imports (.js extension even for .ts files).\n\nWait, Node16 module resolution means:\n- For relative imports: need to include `.js` extension (which maps to `.ts` at compile time)\n- For bare specifiers (npm packages): no extension needed\n\nSo imports would look like:\n```typescript\nimport { db } from './db.js';\nimport { requireAuth } from './middleware.js';\n```\n\nLet me make sure I do this correctly.\n\nOK let me write all the code.\n\nLet me also plan carefully for the middleware. I'll create a TypeScript declaration to extend Express Request.\n\n```typescript\n// middleware.ts\nimport { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';\nimport jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';\n\nconst JWT_SECRET = 'analytics-dashboard-secret';\n\nexport interface JwtPayload {\n  userId: string;\n  email: string;\n  tenantId: string;\n  role: string;\n}\n\ndeclare global {\n  namespace Express {\n    interface Request {\n      user?: JwtPayload;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport function requireAuth(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction): void {\n  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;\n  if (!authHeader || !authHeader.startsWith('Bearer ')) {\n    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication required' });\n    return;\n  }\n  \n  const token = authHeader.slice(7);\n  try {\n    const payload = jwt.verify(token, JWT_SECRET) as JwtPayload;\n    req.user = payload;\n    next();\n  } catch {\n    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' });\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, I should also handle the case where the user might want to pass the middleware. Let me also add optional admin middleware.\n\nOK, I think I have a solid plan. Let me write all the files now. I'll create them in parallel.\n\nLet me make sure `npm install` has been run first.\n\nActually, let me also check if node_modules exists.","signature":"a293432c-4116-44d3-8d95-29d16b0e62d2"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1035,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":20352,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_us